<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ATP biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>|FRAME: EC-7.1.2.2| (previously EC 3.6.3.14) is an enzyme that uses the free energy derived from the transmembrane proton gradient to catalyze the synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. Several types of ATP synthases are known. The F-type is a large enzyme found in mitochondria, chloroplasts and bacteria. The enzyme comprises two subcomplexes - a membrane sector (Fo) and a cytoplasmic-compartment sector (F1). Functionally, ATP synthase may be considered tripartite, consisting of a rotor, a rotor stalk or stator, and three catalytic sites where ATP synthesis or hydrolysis occurs|CITS: [11997128][17662937]|. The enzyme is a rotary molecular nanomotor that couples the mechanical force of subunit rotation to the synthesis or hydrolysis of ATP at the enzyme&apos;s catalytic sites. Proton movement across the membrane, which is driven by the H+ electrochemical potential gradient, generates subunit rotation that drives ATP synthesis by forcing sequential conformational changes at each of the three catalytic sites. The rotor rotates by consecutive 120&amp;deg; angles, and performs parts of the ATP synthesis reaction during rotation. The V-type (in vacuoles and clathrin-coated vesicles) and bacterial P-type (plasma) enzymes have a similar structure but, under physiological conditions, operate in the opposite direction, utilizing energy from ATP hydrolysis to pump protons or other ions across the membrane |CITS: [10600677][ 21179061]|. In facultative organisms such as |FRAME: TAX-562| the enzyme can change its direction according to the growth mode. While the enzyme operates as an ATP synthase during aerobic respiration, it functions in the reverse direction during fermentation, catalyzing the hydrolysis of ATP to generate the electrochemical proton gradient needed for other membrane functions |CITS: [14985119]|.</Description>
